{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Create an opsbot account on node1 and enable node1 to execute commands on node2 as opsbot using an SSH key.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The account and protected RSA key exist on node1, the corresponding authorized key exists on node2, and node1 successfully executed remote commands as opsbot on node2 both before and after both nodes restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f013eed5de354639","cmd-079ae987c3354741","cmd-e1179259535c42d0","cmd-91d972177a5a44ec"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Require key-based authentication for opsbot on both systems.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both accounts have locked passwords, sshd has a validated per-user policy disabling password authentication and enabling public-key authentication, key logins succeeded, and password-only attempts were directly rejected on both systems, including after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f013eed5de354639","cmd-665f83f955c54d7d","cmd-e1179259535c42d0","cmd-f68df2261ad04e39","cmd-a66dc850be174c6a","cmd-91d972177a5a44ec"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Preserve the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained Ubuntu 7.10 with kernel 2.6.22-14-generic. The implementation used the existing OpenSSH 4.6-compatible configuration, validated it with sshd, and remained functional after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c3ba18ae5d0f4f51","cmd-c0181ea5387f4a7c","cmd-f68df2261ad04e39","cmd-a66dc850be174c6a","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:before_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. The temporary configuration tests were causally relevant to legacy OpenSSH compatibility; the two initially failed tests briefly left test files, but later supported-form tests overwrote and removed them. Account, key, sshd, known-host, and restart changes directly served the task or mandatory restart protocol. Final snapshots show no temporary-file residue or package damage. Both restart protocols contain healthy pre-state, acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered SSH/account/key behavior; fixed lifecycle boot identities independently agree.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3817afa0af204174","cmd-3332f92005284846","cmd-0848446f07db4760","cmd-60497f5835794a8b","cmd-cb62872252e445eb","cmd-190e794eaf7f4ea8","cmd-f68df2261ad04e39","cmd-cbd749f8e1bc429b","cmd-d8a681ba4a8e473c","cmd-a66dc850be174c6a","cmd-91d972177a5a44ec","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. Key-only opsbot SSH access works from node1 to node2, password-only access is rejected on both nodes, the legacy OS and kernel are preserved, and the configuration persisted through independently confirmed restarts of both managed nodes.","limitations":["The lifecycle collector could not assess failed services because systemctl is unsupported on this legacy release; direct sshd syntax, process/listener, login, and post-restart checks provide the relevant service evidence instead."]}
